# handleApiView Method

The view handler checks for the `planProgress` view name, looks up the plan by ID, and returns a custom response:

```java
@Override
public ApiResponse handleApiView(String name, JSONObject params) throws ApiException {
    LOGGER.debug("handleApiView {} {}", name, params);

    if (name.equals(VIEW_PLAN_PROGRESS)) {
        AutomationPlan plan = extension.getPlan(params.getInt(PARAM_PLAN_ID));

        if (plan == null) {
            throw new ApiException(Type.DOES_NOT_EXIST);
        }

        return new ProgressResponse(plan);
    }
    throw new ApiException(Type.BAD_VIEW);
}
```

If the plan lookup returns null, it throws a `DOES_NOT_EXIST` exception; unknown view names throw `BAD_VIEW`.

# Custom ApiResponse Class: ProgressResponse

The `ProgressResponse` inner class extends `ApiResponseSet<Object>`.

## Serialization Overview

The response is built from a map produced by `convertPlan`, which populates these keys:

| Key | Source | Notes |
|-----|--------|-------|
| `planId` | `plan.getId()` | Integer |
| `started` | `plan.getStarted()` | ISO-8601 string via `toIso8601` |
| `finished` | `plan.getFinished()` | ISO-8601 string via `toIso8601` |
| `info` | `plan.getProgress().getInfos()` | List of messages |
| `warn` | `plan.getProgress().getWarnings()` | List of messages |
| `error` | `plan.getProgress().getErrors()` | List of messages |

Dates are converted to strings — a null date becomes an empty string, otherwise `"date.toInstant().toString()"` is used.

## JSON Serialization (toJSON)

There is **no explicit `toJSON` override** in `ProgressResponse`. JSON serialization is inherited from the parent `ApiResponseSet<Object>`, which relies on the map passed to the superclass constructor:

```java
public ProgressResponse(AutomationPlan plan) {
    super(VIEW_PLAN_PROGRESS, convertPlan(plan));
    this.progress = plan.getProgress();
}
```

So for JSON, the `planId`, `started`, `finished`, `info`, `warn`, and `error` fields come directly from the map (info/warn/error being lists).

## XML Serialization (toXML)

The class **does override** `toXML`. It first calls the superclass implementation, then rebuilds each message collection as a properly typed list:

```java
@Override
public void toXML(Document doc, Element parent) {
    super.toXML(doc, parent);

    convertProgressMessages(doc, parent, ELEMENT_INFO, progress::getInfos);
    convertProgressMessages(doc, parent, ELEMENT_WARN, progress::getWarnings);
    convertProgressMessages(doc, parent, ELEMENT_ERROR, progress::getErrors);
}
```

The helper removes any existing nodes for that element name, then creates a list element containing one `message` node per entry, escaping control characters:

```java
private static void convertProgressMessages(
        Document doc, Element parent, String elementName, Supplier<List<String>> messages) {
    var nodeList = parent.getElementsByTagName(elementName);
    for (int i = 0; i < nodeList.getLength(); i++) {
        parent.removeChild(nodeList.item(i));
    }

    Element messagesList = doc.createElement(elementName);
    messagesList.setAttribute("type", "list");
    for (String message : messages.get()) {
        Element el = doc.createElement("message");
        el.appendChild(
                doc.createTextNode(
                        message != null ? XMLStringUtil.escapeControlChrs(message) : ""));
        messagesList.appendChild(el);
    }
    parent.appendChild(messagesList);
}
```

## Summary of Field Serialization

- **planId / started / finished**: handled uniformly through the base map for both JSON and XML.
- **info / warn / error**: appear as lists in JSON from the map; in XML they are explicitly rebuilt as `type="list"` elements containing `message` children, with each message run through `XMLStringUtil.escapeControlChrs`. Null messages become empty strings.

